Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||04:22 PM||||Both my lock cylinders are destroyed, one looks like it's had a screwdriver stuck in it. How can I get into the car?? I don't care if the locks need to be removed etc, just as long as I can get in... Is there some trick, or do I need to call a locksmith?<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||8
Z||000001||California Kid||12-08-2001||04:37 PM||||Locksmith is generally cheaper than replacing parts that might get damaged.<BR>Door weatherstrip is not cheap!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever3000||12-08-2001||04:56 PM||||Got a sunroof?<BR>Smack that baby right next to the latch.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||04:59 PM||||no sunroof.. t-tops.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||05:02 PM||||take a 1/4" rod ( 6' long ) and bend 1" at ther end to 90 degrees. slip rod between rear of window and weather stripping and unlock the door using the bent end. get inside, grab keys, and run down to the hardware store and have several copies made.<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||05:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>take a 1/4" rod ( 6' long ) and bend 1" at ther end to 90 degrees. slip rod between rear of window and weather stripping and unlock the door using the bent end. get inside, grab keys, and run down to the hardware store and have several copies made.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have the keys... the door lock cylinders are both trashed. I could probably use a coat hanger too, right?<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||05:17 PM||||possibly, but it just might bend, worst case scenario ( don't let the wife find out ), grab a curtain rod from the living room or of the sort, and bend it to shape to open the door<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||05:19 PM||||damn, to bad your so far away, other wise I'd just drive over to your house and open it for you, kind of like the Fiero Road Side Assistance Service<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||05:22 PM||||Where does the wire go in? right above the lock cylinder?<BR>I've never done this before..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000009||Mach10||12-08-2001||05:34 PM||||No, goes thru the window... You are trying to hook the unlock latch.<P>You would do well to slather a little grease onto the wire, and onto the weatherstrip. That way you won't wreck it.<P>FORTUNATELY, the unlock latch has to be PUSHED, rather than pulled. This makes it considerably easier. Have a friend spot you in the other side of the car, so you don't rip your fabric, and to guide you to the right spot (Up a little, left, left, down, GOT IT! NNnoooooo... Missed it...)<P>Sort of a hobby. I can "break into" all of the cars' our family's owned, except for our Focus. Haven't played with it. I also enjoy picking locks. Best puzzle around: Grab a padlock, and a handful of paper clips, and pick while at work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||05:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>No, goes thru the window... You are trying to hook the unlock latch.<P>You would do well to slather a little grease onto the wire, and onto the weatherstrip. That way you won't wreck it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I meant, does it go between the glass and the weatherstrip, right above the lock?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000011||DXR_DAD||12-08-2001||05:57 PM||||Steve get a "Slim Jim"!!!! You need the 1" bend at the end. Slide it between the glass and dew strip, Just about at the front edge of the door handle!! you need to push the lock rod to the back. It's been awhile but I think thats it?<P>Good luck<BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000012||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||06:04 PM||||I've been there with a slim-jim, perhaps you may have better luck with it, but I have yet to find the "sweet-spot" that will unlock the car. I chose to go the rod route as I have done this before with my car and it alows me to unlock the car in nearly 30 seconds. Yes, the rod goes inbetween the glass and the weather strippingat the rear of the window, down to the lock, push it forward with the rod - bam - your done.<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||12-08-2001||07:00 PM||||Yeah, between the glass and the weatherstrip. The grease is to stop the weatherstrip from catching and ripping.||||reg||7
Z||000014||Adrift||12-08-2001||07:27 PM||||Or, if this is a parts car or something, pop off the door skin and get at the lock that way.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||07:31 PM||||Yes, it's my t-top coupe, which I'm selling for parts..<P>I'll try the wire first, if that doesn't work, I'll see about removing the door skin. how hard is that?<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000016||Loki||12-08-2001||08:23 PM||||Cant be removed from the outside with out ALOT of damge and well destroying the skin...<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieroloki.com/lokisig1%20copy.jpg">   <BR>"All Fieros broke right now" :(||||reg||1
Z||000017||Firebug||12-08-2001||11:16 PM||||On some fiero's (mine) the window can be gently pried away from the weather strip slightly and a flat piece of steel with a 90 degree angle on the bottom (like a 1" finger) can be slid against the "unlock" knob.  Without a doubt, the fiero is the easiest car to break into next to early ford pickups with windwings. (Very small flat screwdriver to open windwing).  The first time will take you 10 min.  The second time will take you 30 sec.<P>Good luck, <BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000018||DaRkLoRD||12-08-2001||11:21 PM||||I thought as much.. but since I have t-tops, I didn't want to risk damaging them by prying the window too much.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||tesmith66||12-10-2001||08:33 AM||||Does the car have power locks? If so, use wooden wedges to hold the glass away from the weatherstrip, and then use a long, wooden dowel or 1/4" steel rod to push the unlock button on the opposite door.<P>...this will (of course) only work if the battery is hooked up...<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||5
Z||000020||WKDFIRO||12-10-2001||11:13 AM||||At Fiero Motorsports we had a particular rod that would be perfect for opening Fieros that had been locked out. The bends were more than perfect. As long as one was careful pulling the glass away the rod would go in and unlock in 10 seconds. We hung it on a hook by the door. <P>The rod was the particular rod that holds the spare tire in place in a Fiero! Perfect length, the L shaped end hooks right in and because its rounded its a little easier on the weatherstripping. Very handy to find on in a junk yard and keep in the garage when you might need it!||||reg||6
Z||000021||stsmithgt||12-10-2001||12:09 PM||||Similar to what tesmith66 said.  I used an aluminum tent pole that was also about ¼ inch in diameter to reach the opposite doors button.  I was out camping is why I had to McGyver the tent pole.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||DaRkLoRD||12-10-2001||05:05 PM||||no power locks, no battery either.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000023||paulmckibben||12-10-2001||10:24 PM||||Below is another method to get into your Fiero courtesy of Ed Parks (The Fiero Factory).  This works, but may take some time to find the right place to push.  Will not damage the paint.<P>- - - - - -<BR>Remove the radio antenna from your Fiero.  Invert.  Approximately 1" from the rear edge of the door, start to "probe" for the door latch bellcrank (from outside of the window).  The knob of the antenna fits perfectly into the latch bellcrank.  In approximately 1 minute to 10 hours you will find the door latch bellcrank to push DOWN.  That will release the door latch.  Happy hunting!!<P>There is no way to remove the inner door panel without the door being open.  And without the door open, the lower rivets of the door panel cannot be removed.  Either requires some destruction.  Slim Jims seldom work on a Fiero.  They are designed to pull up.  The Fiero pushes down.  Ed Parks.<P>- - - - - -<P>You can also remove a door which has a damaged lock (as from an accident) when it cannot be opened from inside or outside.  My Formula was damaged this way in a parking lot.  Some s.o.b. hit the car and ran, leaving the door skin intact but with extensive inner damage that completely jammed the lock mechanism.  In fact the door frame was totalled but the skin was fine.  Here's how to get the door open:<P>Remove the adjacent front fender, pull the door hinge pins or unscrew the hinge bolts, then swing the door out using the locked rear edge as a hinge.  While a helper holds the door open, remove the inner door panel, reach inside the door with a Torx T-55 wrench and unscrew the striker post.  The door is then be completely free from the car even though still "locked".<P>Paul McKibben<BR>Norcross, GA||||reg||1
Z||000024||daved||12-10-2001||11:09 PM||||Er...Steve...I have an extra set of keys for that car, if it will help.  Give me a call.  Did someone try to break into it?||||reg||1
